insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 1, 'Why do k1 and k2 stop working for a deviated well?', '["They assume a contiguous run of layers, and a deviated well may skip over some of the layers entirely as it descends through the structure.", "They assume the well is a vertical line through a single column, and a deviated well crossing columns has no one column to name.", "They carry no length, so the simulator cannot weight the connections that a deviated well makes along its path.", "They are limited to five layers by the record format, which a long lateral in a layered reservoir will exceed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cells a deviated path crosses are not a contiguous run of layers in anything, so the completion has to be an explicit list. Producing that list is a geometry problem rather than a bookkeeping one.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 2, 'What three things does a connection carry beyond its cell indices?', '["A well index, a skin factor and a wellbore radius, which together set the coupling strength to the cell.", "A layer thickness, a permeability and a net-to-gross, read from the grid at the cell the connection sits in.", "A length, a direction and an order.", "A measured depth, an inclination and an azimuth, carried through from the directional survey the path came from."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Length is how much wellbore is in the cell, direction decides which cell dimensions the well index treats as flow length, and order is heel to toe. The well index is computed FROM those rather than carried alongside them.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 3, 'The Ekene side-track runs from (1900, 1800) to (1500, 2100) on a 100 m grid. Roughly how many columns should it cross?', '["Around eight, since it covers 400 m east-west and 300 m north-south, which is a diagonal of about 500 m across cells of 100 m.", "Around five, since only the longer of the two horizontal components determines how many of the column boundaries the path actually crosses.", "Around thirteen, since the crossings in each direction add: four column boundaries east-west plus three north-south.", "It cannot be estimated without the depths, because the number of columns depends on how steeply the path descends."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The estimate is worth making before running any intersector, because it is the number you check the answer against. The deck reports eight distinct columns.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 4, 'How does the intersector find which cells a path crosses?', '["It solves analytically for the intersection of the path with each of the grid''s cell faces in turn.", "It projects the path onto the map, lists the columns it crosses, and then assigns layers from the path''s depths.", "It tests every cell in the grid against the path''s bounding box and keeps the ones the path passes through.", "It walks the path in small steps, finds the cell each step''s midpoint is in, and accumulates length."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Consecutive steps landing in the same cell are merged. The step size is a fraction of the smallest cell dimension, which on this grid is the roughly 6 ft layer thickness, so the walk proceeds in steps of a foot or two.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 5, 'Why must the step size be smaller than the smallest cell dimension?', '["So no cell can be crossed within a single step and therefore missed entirely by the walk.", "So the accumulated lengths are accurate to within a foot, which is the precision a well index calculation needs.", "So consecutive steps always land in adjacent cells, which is what lets the merging logic work correctly.", "So the direction flags can be computed, which requires at least two steps inside each cell to establish a trend."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A step longer than a cell can start on one side and finish on the other, and the cell in between is never sampled. On this grid the layers are the smallest dimension at about 6 ft.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 6, 'The Ekene side-track produces eleven connections across eight columns. Why the mismatch?', '["Three of the columns are visited twice by a path that curves back on itself, and the visits are listed separately.", "Three connections fall in cells the path merely grazes, and those are listed in addition to the eight full crossings.", "Three of the columns are entered in one layer and left in the next, so each of those three contributes two connections rather than one.", "Three of the eight columns carry two completions each because the well was perforated in two intervals there."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Columns (19, 20), (18, 21) and (17, 21) each appear twice in the table, once in one layer and once in the next. The path descends as it goes, and a layer change inside a column produces a second connection.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 7, 'The connection lengths run from 28.81 ft to 257.84 ft. What follows?', '["The 28 ft connection should be dropped, since a connection shorter than a cell dimension is a grazing artifact.", "The 28 ft connection contributes roughly an eighth of what the 257 ft one does, all else equal, because length enters the well index directly.", "The two contribute equally, since the well index depends only on the cell''s own permeability and dimensions rather than on the length of wellbore inside it.", "The ratio bounds the error in the walk, since the shortest connection is the one most sensitive to the step size chosen."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A connection list without lengths would treat every entry as equal and overstate the well''s productivity. The short ones are cells the path clips at a corner; the long ones it runs the full width of.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 8, 'Why is the connection list a numerical result rather than a closed form?', '["Because the grid geometry is only approximately known, so the cell faces the path crosses carry their own uncertainty.", "Because the direction flags are assigned by a majority rule, which has no exact answer when the components are close.", "Because the trajectory itself is integrated from a survey by minimum curvature, which is an approximation to the true path.", "Because it comes from a discretised walk, so the lengths are accurate to about the step size."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Halve the step size and the connection SET is identical while the lengths shift in the second decimal. If the SET changes when you refine the step, the path is grazing a cell boundary, and that is worth knowing about.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 9, 'Every one of the side-track''s eleven connections reads X. Why, given it descends through all five layers?', '["Because within any single cell the horizontal component of the path dominates the vertical by more than an order of magnitude.", "Because the intersector assigns X to every connection in any well that is not purely vertical, reserving the Z flag for vertical wells alone.", "Because the layers are thinner than the cells are wide, so a path crossing a layer must also cross a column boundary.", "Because the direction is taken from the well''s overall heading rather than from its behaviour in each individual cell."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The path covers 500 m horizontally and about 10 m vertically. The intersector assigns the direction the path spent most of its length travelling in, and that is X everywhere. A path dropping 30 ft while moving 30 ft laterally would produce a mixture.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 10, 'Ekene''s vertical permeability is a tenth of its horizontal. What does a connection wrongly flagged Z do?', '["It uses the layer thickness as the flow length where the cell width should be used, which understates the well index.", "It changes nothing on this grid, because the two horizontal permeabilities are equal and the formula is symmetric.", "It uses two horizontal permeabilities where one of them should have been the vertical one, so it overstates the well index substantially.", "It couples the well to the layer above and below as well, because a Z connection is treated as spanning the interfaces."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The error is roughly the square root of the permeability anisotropy. Most simulators default the flag to Z when it is omitted, which makes it one of the most consequential defaults in a deck.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 11, 'The side-track moves 400 m east-west and 300 m north-south. What would make it receive Y flags instead?', '["A grid with anisotropic horizontal permeability, since the flag is assigned to whichever of the two directions carries the higher permeability.", "A toe placed so that the north-south component exceeded the east-west one, since the larger of the two horizontal components wins.", "A steeper descent, since the vertical component would then compete with the horizontal ones for the assignment.", "Nothing on this trajectory, since the flag is fixed by the well''s azimuth as recorded in the directional survey."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It is a genuinely close call at 400 against 300, and worth knowing it was close. Since this grid''s two horizontal permeabilities are equal the flip would change nothing here, and on an anisotropic field it would.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 12, 'A trajectory enters the same cell twice. What does the intersector do, and why?', '["It merges the visits into one connection whose length is the total, because a cell has one pressure and a well one pressure at that point.", "It keeps both visits as separate connections, because each represents a distinct interval of open wellbore.", "It discards the second visit, because the connection order is defined by first entry and a repeat would break it.", "It refuses the trajectory, because a path that re-enters a cell indicates a survey error that should be corrected."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two connections to the same cell would be two couplings to the same pair, which double-counts the flow path. Simulators do accept duplicate COMPDAT records for a cell, and the result is exactly that double coupling.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 13, 'What does merging cost?', '["Accuracy in the lengths, since accumulating across separated visits blurs the position of the wellbore in the cell.", "The direction flags, since a cell visited twice in different orientations has no single direction to report.", "The heel-to-toe ordering, since a merged connection has no single position in the sequence along the wellbore.", "The list no longer tells you the shape of the path."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The connection list is not a trajectory and cannot be inverted back into one, which matters to anyone trying to reconstruct a path from a deck after the survey has been lost. The order is preserved as the order of FIRST entry.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 14, 'What two checks belong on any deviated well''s connection list?', '["That every connection carries a length and a direction, and that the directions are consistent along the path.", "That the connection count matches the column count, and that the layers form a monotonic sequence heel to toe.", "That the summed connection length agrees with the length of trajectory inside the grid, and that no cell appears in the list twice.", "That the first connection sits at the heel depth, and that the last sits at or above the base of the column."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A large shortfall in total length means part of the path fell outside the model. A duplicate cell means the list was assembled by hand or by a tool that does not merge, and the well is double-coupled there.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-trajectories', 15, 'The grid''s shallowest top is 5055.774278215223 ft and its deepest base 5251.120588883762 ft. What check does that enable?', '["Confirm the trajectory''s toe lies below the shallowest of the column tops, which is the condition for the well to have reached the reservoir at all.", "Compare the trajectory''s depth range against that interval before intersecting, since a path that does not overlap it cannot connect.", "Confirm the connection count is at least as large as the number of layers the interval spans at the well''s position.", "Confirm the heel and toe depths bracket the contact, which is the condition for the well to be completed in oil."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It is two numbers and a comparison, and it catches both the depth reference trap and the unit trap before the intersector runs. A survey in metres fed into this grid would sit at roughly a third of its true depth, well above the sand.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 1, 'What separates WCONHIST from WCONPROD?', '["WCONHIST applies to producers under rate control and WCONPROD to producers under pressure control.", "WCONHIST carries one record per well per period and WCONPROD carries one record per well for the whole run.", "WCONPROD says what a well is told to do; WCONHIST says what a well did.", "WCONHIST is written before the DATES that closes its period and WCONPROD is written after the one that opens it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The difference shows in what the simulator does with the extra numbers. Under a target and limits a well produces the lesser of them; under observations it honours the phase the control mode names and records the rest for comparison.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 2, 'Under ORAT control, what does the model reproduce by construction and what must it predict?', '["It reproduces the oil rate exactly, and must predict the water cut, the gas-oil ratio and the bottom-hole pressure.", "It reproduces the total liquid rate exactly, and must then predict the oil-water split at surface and the reservoir pressure.", "It reproduces the reservoir voidage exactly, and must predict all three surface phase rates independently.", "It reproduces nothing by construction, since ORAT sets a target the well meets only if its inflow allows it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The oil rate is imposed rather than matched, so a plot of modelled against observed oil under ORAT control is a straight line through the origin by definition. The free responses are the only ones carrying evidence.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 3, 'Which control mode would suit a waterflood whose ledger is trusted on voidage?', '["ORAT, because oil is the best-measured stream in any waterflood and the ledger''s voidage follows from it.", "RESV, because it honours reservoir volume and leaves the surface phase rates free.", "LRAT, because voidage is a total-liquid quantity once the formation volume factors are applied to it.", "None, because voidage cannot be imposed as a well control and must be matched by adjusting the injection instead."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each mode makes a different quantity exact and leaves the others free, so the choice decides what the match is testing. LRAT suits a well on a pump with known liquid capacity; Ekene uses ORAT.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 4, 'A well was down for two months. What is the difference between recording SHUT and recording OPEN with zero rates?', '["A shut well is removed from the output vectors entirely, while an open well at zero rate still reports a rate of zero.", "A shut well retains the previous period''s rates through keyword persistence, while an open well recorded at zero explicitly overrides them instead.", "There is no difference in the model; the distinction exists only so the history file records why the well stopped.", "A shut well is disconnected; an open well at zero rate is one the simulator tries to produce at zero, which constrains its pressure."]'::jsonb, 3, 'They are different models and the second is usually what happened. Constraining a well to produce zero imposes a condition on the cell pressure that a genuinely disconnected well does not.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 5, 'Ekene-1 produced 998.5247575266284 stb in January 2023. What rate does the schedule carry, and how is it obtained?', '["32.210476049246076 stb/d, from dividing by January''s own 31 days rather than by any average month.", "32.804 stb/d, from dividing by the mean month of 30.4375 days the prediction tail also uses.", "998.5247575266284 stb/d, because WCONHIST accepts a monthly volume directly when the history is monthly.", "32.210476049246076 stb/d, from dividing by 31 days and then correcting for the well''s uptime in the month."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The days come from the actual calendar. There is no uptime correction: a rate that reproduces the observed volume over the period is what the schedule wants, whether the well ran all month or not.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 6, 'The deck''s 36-month oil total is 176923.83644033302 stb against the ledger''s 176923.83644033293. What does that difference mean?', '["That one month''s rate was rounded on its way into the schedule, which is why the totals differ in the last digits.", "That the ledger includes a well the deck does not, contributing a small volume in the final significant figures.", "Nothing beyond the order the floating-point additions were performed in, since the two sums accumulate the same values differently.", "That the deck''s periods span slightly different intervals than the ledger''s months, accumulating a tiny drift."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ledger sums per well per month; the deck sums rate times days per period. Floating-point addition is not associative. A difference at the fifteenth significant figure is arithmetic and one at the fourth is a bug.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 7, 'What does the round trip check on a history schedule consist of?', '["Re-deriving each month''s volume from the simulator''s output vectors and comparing against the schedule''s rates.", "Multiplying each rate by the days its period spans, summing over wells and periods, and comparing against the ledger''s own total.", "Comparing the schedule''s first and last dates against the ledger''s, confirming the two cover the same interval.", "Confirming that the number of rate blocks equals the number of dates, which catches a dropped or duplicated period."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It catches every divisor mistake at once and costs one line. It requires no simulator run, which is the point: the check is on the deck rather than on the results.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 8, 'Why is a mean-month divisor''s symptom distinctive?', '["Because the annual total is correct while the monthly pattern is wrong, which is unique to a divisor error.", "Because the error is exactly 1.8 percent in every month, which is a signature no other mistake produces.", "Because it appears only in the gas rates, since the oil rates are re-derived by the simulator from the observed volumes.", "Because the residuals correlate with month length, which nothing else does."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every 31-day month is over-produced and every 30-day month under-produced, and February by nearly ten percent. A history match with that pattern has this bug and nothing else.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 9, 'The ledger holds gas in Mscf. What check catches a ledger that was actually in scf?', '["The gas-oil ratio: at 400 scf/stb a well making 32 stb/d should make about 12.9 Mscf/d, so a number near 12900 is the unit error.", "The round trip check, since multiplying the rate back by the days would give a thousand times the ledger''s volume.", "The validator, which refuses a gas rate more than three orders of magnitude above the field''s expected production.", "The material balance, since a gas rate a thousand times too large would exhaust the whole of the modelled gas cap within a single period."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The round trip would pass, because both the rate and the volume would be scaled by the same thousand. Only an independent ratio catches it.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 10, 'In the schedule''s pattern, what defines the period a rate block covers?', '["The DATES block that precedes it, which opens the period and therefore sets the moment from which the rates take effect.", "The TSTEP length in force at that point, since a rate block applies for exactly one report step.", "The DATES block that FOLLOWS it, which closes the period and therefore defines the interval the rates apply over.", "Neither; each WCONHIST record carries its own period length as the last item in the record."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A rate block applies from the current time to the NEXT date. Reading it the other way round shifts the whole history by a month, which is nearly invisible on a cumulative plot and obvious on a rate plot.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 11, 'Ekene''s history ends 2025-12 and its final DATES advances to 2026-01-01. What if that final date were omitted?', '["The last month would simply be dropped, and the deck''s cumulative production would fall one month short of the ledger.", "The last month''s rates would run open-ended into whatever came next, so the final history month would last five years.", "The simulator would refuse the deck, since a rate block with no closing date has an undefined period length.", "The prediction tail would start a month early, shifting every forecast report step back by one calendar month."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The prediction tail is 60 TSTEPs of 30.4375 days, so an unclosed final history period runs across all of it. The counts check catches this: 36 rate blocks against 35 dates is an unclosed period.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 12, 'The validator enforces one explicit rule on the first history period. What is it?', '["It must contain at least one producer, since a history opening with injection alone has no oil to match against.", "It must be at least as long as the shortest prediction step, so the simulator''s first timestep is not truncated.", "It must precede the first DATES block, since a history period defined after a date advance would be empty.", "Its date must equal the deck''s start date."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A mismatch is silent otherwise: the simulator would run from the start date with no wells until the first period, which is a shut-in field nobody asked for. Ekene starts 2023-01-01 and its first period is 2023-01-01.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 13, 'What does Eclipse keyword persistence do to a history built from a database with gaps?', '["It carries the last known rate straight across the gap, so a row missing from the source data silently becomes a repeated one.", "It produces nothing across the gap, so a missing row becomes a shut-in period the ledger never recorded.", "It interpolates across the gap between the surrounding periods, smoothing the rate through the missing months.", "It raises a warning naming the well and the period, which is why every deck should be run with warnings enabled."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The defence is to write every well in every period, even when the rate is unchanged, so a missing row is a missing row. That is what makes the block counts a meaningful check.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 14, 'What changes at the history-to-prediction join, and what does not?', '["The control basis and the fluid description change; the grid and the well completions carry across untouched.", "The time stepping and the report frequency change; the well controls and the observed rates both carry across the join untouched.", "The control basis and the time stepping change; the pressures, saturations and cumulative volumes carry across untouched.", "Nothing changes but the keywords used, since WCONPROD with targets equal to the last observed rates is continuous."]'::jsonb, 2, 'History advances on calendar dates because the data has dates; prediction advances on uniform mean months because nothing in it is tied to a calendar. That is why report steps stop lining up with month ends.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-history-schedule', 15, 'The last observed rate is about 32 stb/d and the forecast target is 1500 stb/d. What does the output show?', '["A smooth continuation, since the simulator ramps a well from its last rate to its new target over the first timestep.", "A jump at the join followed by a fall back to whatever the reservoir and the pressure limit allow, which is an artifact of the deck rather than an event.", "A jump at the join that persists, since the well is now under a target it will meet until the reservoir pressure fails.", "No change at all, since a target well above the well''s deliverability is never reached and the well stays at 32 stb/d."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene''s prediction uses round numbers because it is illustrative rather than a real forecast, and the study should say so. A plot running history and forecast together without marking the join invites a reader to read the jump as physical.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 1, 'What distinguishes a calibration from a validation?', '["A validation compares against a measurement and a calibration against another model''s output, so only the first uses data.", "A validation is performed once at the end of a study and a calibration is repeated whenever new data arrives.", "A validation is a test the model could fail; a calibration is an adjustment the model cannot fail, only be tuned through.", "A validation adjusts several parameters simultaneously while a calibration adjusts exactly one against one target."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both are legitimate. Confusing them is not, because a study that calls a calibration a validation believes it has an independent check when it has none.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 2, 'What is calibrated in this model, and against what?', '["The kriging regional mean, calibrated against the NG5 volumetric booking of 12139208.107496763 stock tank barrels.", "The contact depth, against the deepest oil and shallowest water observed in the six wells that penetrated the sand.", "The variogram range, against the residuals between the interpolated surface and the six mapped well tops.", "The porosity, against the volumetric booking, since porosity is the only parameter both models share directly."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The regional mean is the depth the top surface reverts to far from any well. It is a free parameter in the strict sense: nothing anywhere in the field''s dataset says what it should be.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 3, 'Why is the regional mean the defensible parameter to tune?', '["Because it enters the volume linearly, which makes the search converge quickly and predictably to a unique answer.", "Because it affects the shape of the surface only where there is no data at all, so tuning it overrides no measurement anywhere.", "Because it is the only one of the kriging parameters a simulator''s grid is sensitive to at all, the range and sill affecting only smoothness.", "Because it was never measured on this field, unlike the variogram, which was fitted to the six well tops directly."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The wells are measurements, the contact is mapped, and the porosity, saturation and formation volume factor came from earlier courses. The variogram''s range and sill do affect the shape between wells, which the wells themselves partly constrain.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 4, 'Why is the booked volume the right target rather than the deck''s?', '["Because it was computed first, and a later model should be brought into line with an earlier published number.", "Because it carries more significant figures, having been computed on a finer frame than the simulation grid uses.", "Because it is the number the field''s reserves are booked against, and a model disagreeing with it cannot be published.", "Because it came from a workflow built for volumetrics on a frame chosen for that purpose."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A mapped structure, a mapped isochore, a contact and a volumetric engine. The simulation model''s structure exists so a simulator can flow fluid through it, and its far-field shape serves no purpose at all.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 5, 'Why would adjusting porosity to match the volume be indefensible?', '["Because porosity is a measured quantity that appears in the booking as well, so moving it overrides a measurement with a preference.", "Because porosity enters the volume through the same product as saturation, so the two cannot be separated by one target.", "Because the simulator recomputes porosity from the grid geometry when it initialises, so any adjustment made to it would simply be discarded.", "Because porosity is a cell property rather than a surface property, and a single scalar cannot be tuned against a volume."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rule is to calibrate the unconstrained parameter against the constrained quantity, never the reverse. A model with adjusted porosity would be wrong in every calculation that uses it, which is most of them.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 6, 'The deck''s oil in place is a monotonically decreasing function of the regional mean. Why does that matter?', '["Because it guarantees the calibrated answer is unique, so no other regional mean reproduces the booked volume.", "Because it means the sensitivity of volume to the mean is constant, so the residual can be corrected in one step.", "Because monotonicity is exactly the condition a bisection search needs.", "Because it makes the objective differentiable, which is what lets a gradient method find the answer efficiently."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Move the mean shallower and the surface floats up: more columns rise above the contact and the model holds more oil. Bracket with a shallow mean giving too much and a deep one giving too little, then bisect.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 7, 'The calibrated regional mean is 1570.026311 m TVD. Why is the resulting match not exact?', '["Because the bisection was stopped at a tolerance rather than run to machine precision, which would close the gap.", "Because the objective is a step function: columns and layers cross the contact one at a time, so the volume changes in jumps.", "Because the two models use different frames, so a residual of the order of one cell is irreducible by any search.", "Because the booking''s own value carries rounding of its own, so matching it any more closely than a tenth of a percent would be meaningless."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The residual is the size of the smallest step the model can take rather than a failure of the search. Reporting the match as within a tenth of a percent rather than as an exact figure is the honest phrasing.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 8, 'Which three sanity checks does the lesson apply to the calibrated answer?', '["That it converged, that the residual is below tolerance, and that repeating the search from a different bracket agrees.", "That it reproduces the six well tops, that it honours the contact, and that the resulting surface closes structurally.", "That it is deeper than the contact, that it is shallower than the deepest well, and that it lies within the mapped area.", "That it is physically reasonable, that it lies in the interior of the bracket, and that the volume responds in the expected direction."]'::jsonb, 3, '1570 m is about 10 m below the contact and a little below the deepest well, which found the sand at 1590 m. A calibrated mean of 1400 m, shallower than every well, would have signalled that something else was wrong.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 9, 'One free parameter was used. What does that bound?', '["It can match one number, which is the whole content of having one degree of freedom.", "It bounds the residual, since a single parameter can always be driven to reproduce a target to machine precision.", "It bounds the uncertainty, since a model with one free parameter carries less structural uncertainty than one with several.", "It bounds the search cost, since a one-dimensional objective is the only kind a bisection can be applied to at all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It cannot simultaneously match the oil area, the crest depth and the volume. Matching more than one quantity needs more than one parameter, and then the usual questions about identifiability apply.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 10, 'The booking counts 169 oil cells and the deck 266, with the volumes agreeing. What causes that?', '["The deck''s frame is finer than the booking''s, so the same accumulation is represented by a proportionally larger number of cells.", "The deck includes the columns the two injectors sit in, which the booking excluded as lying below the mapped contact.", "The booking clips area at the contact and gives every cell a full isochore; the deck clips by cell centre and most of its columns are partial.", "The booking counts only columns carrying the full five layers of oil, while the deck counts every column with any oil at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Matching the total means the larger area compensates for the thinner average column. One equation, one unknown: fix the volume and the area follows.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 11, 'What is the deck''s average oil column thickness, given 34.6 ft over 169 cells matched by 266?', '["About 55 ft, from 34.6 multiplied by 266 over 169, since the deck spreads the same oil over more cells.", "About 22 ft, from 34.6 multiplied by 169 over 266.", "About 34.6 ft, since the layer thicknesses are the same in both models and only the areal extent differs.", "It cannot be computed from those numbers alone, because the deck''s cells have a different areal footprint."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The deck''s oil is spread over 57 percent more area at roughly two thirds the average thickness. Both models use the same 100 m cells, which is what makes the ratio a straight one.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 12, 'What does the broader, thinner accumulation cost the model?', '["A lower initial pressure, since the thinner columns hold less oil above the datum and the equilibration reflects that.", "A coarser vertical resolution, since the same layering must now describe a column two thirds of its original thickness.", "A slower simulation, since 266 active oil cells carry more unknowns per timestep than the booking''s 169 would.", "Oil in a fringe the flood will not reach, more contact with the water leg, and a recovery factor not comparable with the booking''s."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The extra 97 cells are the thin fringe at the edge of the accumulation, far from the wells. A recovery factor from this model is a fraction of a differently distributed volume.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 13, 'Calibrating on cell count instead of volume would give exactly 169 oil cells. What would the model then hold?', '["Roughly half the booked volume, because most of those 169 cells would be partial columns rather than full ones.", "Roughly the booked volume, since matching the area and using the same isochore reproduces the same integral.", "Roughly twice the booked volume, since a tighter structure concentrates the oil into fuller columns near the crest.", "The same volume as calibrating on volume does, since the two objectives have the same solution on a monotonic surface."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every forecast from that model would be low by a factor of two, because a forecast is a fraction of the oil in place. That is the decisive argument for matching volume.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 14, 'Which five things belong in the reconciliation write-up?', '["The target, the parameter, the search method, the number of iterations and the final residual of the bisection.", "The structure, the fluid, the rock curves, the wells and the schedule, with a provenance line for each of the five.", "The two volumes and the gap between them, the convention each one used, what was held fixed, what was tuned, and what the tuning cost.", "The booking, the deck, the frame, the engine version and the date, so the comparison can be reproduced exactly."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Without the conventions the two numbers are not comparable and the agreement means nothing. The list of what was held fixed is what makes the residual attributable.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-calibration', 15, 'Which word should not appear, and what would earn it?', '["Calibrated, and it would need the tuning to have been carried out against a direct measurement rather than against another model''s own output.", "Validated, and it would need something the model was not tuned to reproduce, such as a seventh well or an unused pressure survey.", "Matched, and it would need the residual to be zero rather than merely inside a tenth of a percent of the target.", "Independent, and it would need the booking to have been produced by a second engineer working without access to the deck."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The claim will be believed, which is why the distinction is worth defending even when a reviewer pushes back. Ekene has nothing spare: all six wells went into the interpolation.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 1, 'What do all the validator''s rules have in common?', '["Every one is arithmetic, so the validator can run without any knowledge of the format''s keyword semantics.", "Every one of them is about whether the deck can be built and read at all, and none is about whether what it says is right.", "Every one is cross-referential, checking one part of the specification against another rather than in isolation.", "Every one of them is derived from the simulator''s own error list, so any deck that validates is one the simulator is certain to accept."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It will accept a grid in the wrong place, a fluid from the wrong field, a contact 100 ft too deep and a history from a different well, provided each is structurally well formed. It is a grammar check with some arithmetic.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 2, 'Why are the history checks the most valuable of the validator''s rules?', '["Because they are cross-referential: a well named in the schedule but absent from the model is exactly what a human review misses and a machine catches instantly.", "Because the history is the largest part of the deck, so an error there is statistically the most likely to occur.", "Because a history error changes the model''s answer while a grid or fluid error usually only changes its appearance.", "Because the history is the only part of the deck derived from measurements, so its errors cannot be found any other way."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The presence checks catch omissions, which are common and obvious. The geometry and well checks catch things that are individually plausible and jointly impossible.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 3, 'Why does a deck builder have only errors while a simulator has warnings too?', '["Because a builder runs before any deck text exists, and a warning has nothing to point at until the text is written.", "Because a simulator''s warnings concern numerical behaviour during the run, which a builder cannot anticipate.", "Because a builder is upstream with a specification in hand, so refusing costs the caller only a corrected input.", "Because a builder is written for one specification format while a simulator must accept several, and warnings absorb the differences."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A simulator is downstream of everybody: decks written by hand, generated by six tools and edited by people who have left. If it refused everything unusual it would refuse most real decks, so it warns and the burden moves to the reader.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 4, 'The fixture carries seven deliberately broken specifications. Why does isolation matter?', '["Because the point of the set is to say something about the validator: seven cases each raising one error shows seven independent rules each firing on its own trigger.", "Because a case raising several errors would leave the validator''s message order undefined, which makes the test fragile.", "Because the seven rules are checked in sequence, so a case tripping an early rule prevents the later ones from running at all.", "Because a specification with more than one fault cannot be repaired incrementally, which is what the exercise asks the reader to do."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Seven cases raising a hundred errors between them tells you only that broken things are broken. The set exists to demonstrate the validator rather than to demonstrate how badly a specification can be mangled.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 5, 'Six of the seven cases raise exactly one error. Which raises two, and why?', '["The missing title, because the title is used to name the deck file as well as to head the RUNSPEC section.", "The missing start date, because a deck with no start date also fails the rule that the first history period must match it.", "The well outside the grid, because a well carrying invalid indices also has a completion that cannot possibly be inside the layers.", "The single-node PVT table, because a table with one node also fails the requirement for a finite datum pressure."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That coupling is real rather than an artifact: the two rules genuinely depend on the same field. The generator asserts that no case raises more than two errors, so a future case that cascades fails the build.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 6, 'The well cases first raised 180 errors each. What had gone wrong?', '["The trajectory intersector had been run before the grid dimensions were validated, so every connection fell outside the grid.", "The completion index had been raised above the layer count, which invalidates every one of the connections in every period of the history.", "The error accumulator had not been reset between cases, so each case inherited the errors raised by all the cases before it.", "The whole wells list had been replaced by one modified well, so the 36-period history named five wells the model no longer contained."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fix was to mutate one well in place and leave the others alone. The same problem hit the layer count case: changing nz invalidated every completion, so the fix was to drop a layer entry and leave nz alone.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 7, 'What is the general lesson from the cascading negative tests?', '["A negative test should assert on the error count rather than on the message, since messages change and counts do not.", "Negative tests should be built from the smallest valid specification upward rather than by breaking a complete one.", "When you build a set of negative tests, check that each one of them fails for the reason you actually think it does.", "A test suite should run its negative cases before its positive ones, so a cascading failure is visible immediately."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A cascading case still fails, so a suite of them still passes, and nobody notices that six of the seven rules are never exercised on their own. The suite looks thorough and tests one thing repeatedly.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 8, 'What two properties do the validator''s messages have that are worth demanding generally?', '["They name the well, and they quote the values that disagree.", "They are ordered by severity, and they stop at the first error rather than accumulating a list the reader must triage.", "They cite the rule number, and they point at the line of the specification file where the offending value was found.", "They suggest a correction, and they state whether the deck would still run with the offending value left in place."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A message naming a well is actionable on a field with seven of them. The history message gives both dates, so a reader can see which one is wrong without opening the deck.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 9, 'Which of these is a warning rather than an error?', '["A completion below the deepest layer, since the well is still declared and the simulator can proceed without it.", "A pressure outside a table''s range, held at the end value.", "A history period whose date precedes the one before it, since the simulator can sort the periods into order itself.", "A missing datum pressure, since the simulator can equilibrate from the datum depth and the fluid contacts alone."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A warning means the deck can be processed and something about it is unusual. Extrapolation is legal and it is exactly the class that changes an answer materially without stopping anything.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 10, 'What is the practical response to a log full of warnings?', '["Configure the simulator to promote the dangerous classes to errors, so a run cannot complete while they are present.", "Read them in full on every run, since a warning that has always been there may still be the one that matters.", "Suppress the classes known to be harmless, so the log stays short enough that every remaining line gets read.", "Triage rather than heroism: read them once on a new deck, decide which classes are expected, and then watch for anything new."]'::jsonb, 3, 'There are hundreds and most are harmless, so reading them all is expensive and reading none is cheap. A warning that appears after a change is worth more than one that has always been there.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 11, 'Which warning class is the dangerous one?', '["Warnings that repeat every timestep, since the volume of them buries the ones that appear only once.", "Warnings about unusual but perfectly legal values, since a porosity of 0.45 or a permeability of 20 darcies is far more often a typo than a measurement.", "Warnings about things you did not know you were doing, such as a defaulted three-phase model or a defaulted direction flag on a horizontal well.", "Warnings issued during the prediction tail, since nothing downstream checks a forecast against an observation."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each of those changes the answer materially and each is a warning rather than an error, because each is legal. A defaulted field is invisible in the deck text, which is what makes the warning the only trace of it.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 12, 'A structure has the correct relief with the crest in the wrong corner. Which comparison catches it?', '["Posting the wells on the model''s structure and checking against the logs.", "Comparing the model''s oil in place against the volumetric booking with the conventions stated on both sides.", "Multiplying the schedule''s rates back by the period lengths and comparing against the production database.", "Comparing the model''s fluid, layer column and rock curves against the tank model, the sweep analysis and the SCAL fit."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every summary statistic matches a transposed map, which is why nothing numerical inside the file finds it. The well posting catches transposition, offsets and unit errors in one look.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 13, 'What error category do none of the four comparisons catch?', '["A history that reproduces the production database exactly, where the database''s own allocation was wrong.", "A model right in every respect and built on a field that does not behave as it assumes, such as one saturation region where there are three.", "A PVT table taken from a correlation where the rest of the study used a designed fluid description.", "A net thickness supplied where gross was intended, which leaves the pore volume out by the net-to-gross."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is a modelling error rather than a deck error, and the only thing that surfaces it is a prediction that fails. Which is an argument for making predictions that can fail, and for making them early.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 14, 'Which two classes of keyword give a deck reach beyond describing a reservoir?', '["Well controls and actions, since both can change the model''s behaviour partway through a run without a new deck.", "Summary requests and report controls, since both write files to paths the deck itself specifies.", "Grid modification and region assignment, since both can alter cell properties after the grid has been read.", "Embedded scripting and file inclusion, taking in the import and restart keywords that share inclusion''s reach."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Neither is a defect. Each is a designed feature that assumes the deck''s author and the deck''s runner are the same person or trust each other, and decks travel.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-validation', 15, 'What should a hosted service do with the scripting keywords?', '["Sandbox them in a container with no network access, so the code can run without reaching anything outside the deck.", "Strip them from the deck before running it, so the run proceeds with the reservoir description the deck also carries.", "Reject them outright rather than attempting to sandbox them, refusing the whole deck with an explanation of why.", "Permit them for decks uploaded by verified organisations, since the trust assumption the format makes then holds."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The other rules are to confine every path inside the deck''s own directory, run with a scrubbed environment holding no credentials, and bound the run by memory, CPU and wall clock. Running somebody else''s deck is running somebody else''s instructions.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 1, 'Why does this module grade nothing?', '["Because the engines the course rests on emit decks and do geometry rather than solving flow equations, so there is no simulated result to check an answer against.", "Because results literacy is a reading skill rather than a computational one, and reading skills cannot be tested by a numeric field.", "Because the results a learner would produce depend on the simulator they happen to use, so no single answer could be marked correct.", "Because the module is optional, sitting outside the six that the tier''s capstone draws its fields from."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A course should grade what it can check and teach what a practitioner needs. This module is the second of those, and it is the same treatment the waterflood course gave to enhanced recovery screening.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 2, 'What is a summary file, and what is it not?', '["A record of every solver step the run took; it is not a report of the model state, which is written separately at selected times.", "A complete record of the run''s own inputs and outputs; it is not readable at all without the deck that produced it, since that is what defines its column names.", "A time series database of one row per report step and one column per requested vector; it is not the full model state, which lives in restart files.", "A per-cell snapshot at selected times; it is not a time series, which has to be assembled by reading several summary files in sequence."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Restart files hold every cell''s pressure and saturation and are enormous. That size difference decides how they are used: summaries are read constantly, restarts when somebody needs to see a map.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 3, 'Ekene''s deck writes 36 history steps and 60 prediction steps. How many summary rows, and what is a row?', '["96 rows, each the state at one of the solver''s own internal steps, of which there is one per report step.", "96 rows, each holding the state at the end of one report step, which is either a DATES entry or a TSTEP interval.", "36 rows, since only the history periods are reported in it and the prediction tail is written out to a separate file of its own.", "It depends on the run, since the simulator adds rows whenever it cuts a timestep to achieve convergence."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The solver''s own internal steps do not appear. A report step may have taken twenty solver steps or one, and the summary shows the state at the end of it either way.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 4, 'How do rates and totals differ in a summary file?', '["A rate is instantaneous at the end of the step; a total is the sum of the rates over all steps to that point.", "A rate is computed by the reporting layer; a total is computed by the solver, so the two use different step lengths.", "A rate carries the units the deck declared; a total is always written in the simulator''s own internal unit set.", "A rate is the average over the report step; a total is the cumulative to that instant."]'::jsonb, 3, 'So differencing consecutive totals and dividing by the step length reproduces the rate exactly, while reading a rate as instantaneous is an approximation that gets worse as steps get longer. If the two disagree in a way that matters, trust the total.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 5, 'What is the highest-value single line in a run''s output?', '["The oil in place at initialisation, read against the volumetric booking.", "The field oil production total at the last report step, which is the number the study exists to forecast.", "The average field pressure at the end of history, which is where a material balance error would first appear.", "The count of warnings, which tells a reader how much of the deck the simulator had to interpret on its own."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That number is the denominator of every recovery factor the study will quote and the first place a static-model error shows up. It is reported before any timestep and reading it takes ten seconds.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 6, 'Decode FGIT, WWCT, FPR and WOPRH.', '["Field gas injection total, well water cut total, field production rate, and well oil production rate at the heel.", "Field gas in place total, well water cut, field pressure ratio, and well oil production rate history-matched.", "Field gas injection total, well water cut, field average pressure, and the observed rather than simulated well oil production rate.", "Field gross injection total, well water cut, field pressure, and well oil production rate at reservoir conditions."]'::jsonb, 2, 'First letter is the scope, F for field and W for well; then the quantity; then a trailing H for the observed value rather than the simulated one.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 7, 'Which vector tells you whether a well was on rate control or on its pressure limit?', '["WOPR against WOPRH, since a well on its pressure limit falls short of the rate it was told to produce.", "WBHP, the well bottom-hole pressure.", "FPR, since a field average pressure below the well''s limit means every well in it must be pressure-constrained.", "WWCT, since a well that reaches its water cut limit is switched off rate control by the simulator automatically."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It is the one people forget and need most: without it a well that fell short of target is unexplained. Comparing WOPR against WOPRH shows that the well fell short but not why.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 8, 'Why does the deck request per-well water cut as well as field water cut?', '["Because the field water cut is computed from the field rates, which are averaged over the whole report step and therefore lose all the within-step variation.", "Because the observed field water cut is an allocated quantity while the per-well values are measured, so only the latter can be matched.", "Because the water cut limit that ends a well''s life is applied per well, so the field value cannot show when a well will be shut in.", "Because a field water cut of 30 percent could be four wells at 30 or two at 5 and two at 55, which are different floods with different remedies."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Aggregate vectors hide exactly the variation that decides what to do. That is precisely the lesson the waterflood course built its whole Professional tier on.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 9, 'What would region vectors add that no field vector can?', '["Where the oil came from and which compartment is depleting, since a region is a set of cells with its own oil in place, pressure and production.", "A per-layer breakdown of the flood front''s position, which is what the layered sweep analysis needs to be checked against.", "A per-well drainage volume, since each region can be defined as the set of cells a given well is connected to.", "A finer time resolution, since region vectors are written at every solver step rather than only at report steps."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene''s deck requests none, and on a bigger study they are the most useful vectors in the file. Defining regions is a GRID-section job and it is cheap, so a study expecting to ask that question should define them before the first run.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 10, 'What is SPE1 and why is its middle layer interesting?', '["A published water injection benchmark whose middle layer carries the highest permeability, so the flood overrides through it.", "A single-well test problem whose middle layer holds the only completion, so the well''s inflow is controlled by one layer alone.", "The first SPE comparative solution project, a ten by ten by three gas injection case whose 50 md middle layer is a barrier the injected gas must break through to reach the producer.", "A history matching benchmark whose middle layer is the only one with observations, so the match is under-determined by design."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The producer sits in the corner at (10, 10) in the bottom layer and the gas injector at the opposite corner (1, 1) in the TOP layer. Exactly when the gas breaks through is sensitive to how a simulator handles the transition, which is what makes the comparison informative.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 11, 'What do the nine published SPE1 curves tell a reader?', '["Which of the nine simulators is the most accurate, since the published envelope is centred on the reference solution.", "How much simulators legitimately disagree with each other on a problem this well specified, which is more than most people expect.", "How sensitive the answer is to timestep size, since the nine runs used different step controls on the same problem.", "That gas injection into an undersaturated oil is inherently unstable, since curves sharing the same overall shape still diverge over time."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is a useful antidote to treating any single run as the answer. The other use of the set is verification: a new simulator landing outside the published envelope has a bug.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 12, 'What is the reference specification''s role in this course''s engine set?', '["It is the fixture the course''s own capstone values are computed against, since Ekene has no published counterpart.", "It is a regression baseline, so a change to the emitter that alters any deck''s output is caught before release.", "It is the validation case for the grid geometry code, since its three uniform layers make the volumetrics analytic.", "It is a check on the deck FORMAT rather than on any physics."]'::jsonb, 3, 'SPE1 expressed in the same form as the Ekene deck is the literature anchor for the emission code: if the emitter can express SPE1 and a simulator accepts it, the emitter is producing decks a simulator understands. That is the right scope for a deck-emission engine.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 13, 'Under ORAT control, what does a plot of FOPR against FOPRH show?', '["Two identical curves, because the model was told the oil rate at every step, so the plot is evidence of nothing whatever.", "Two curves agreeing closely wherever the model matches and diverging where it does not, which is the whole point of plotting the pair together.", "One curve, because the simulator writes the observed vector only when it differs from the simulated one.", "Two curves diverging late in history, because the imposed rate becomes unachievable once the reservoir pressure falls."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The H vectors are not simulator output; they are the numbers you put in the schedule, echoed back so they can be plotted together. That plot appears in a great many reports as evidence of a good history match.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 14, 'What carries the information in Ekene''s history match, and what is missing?', '["Oil and water carry it, because both are metered at surface; gas is missing, because the ledger''s gas is an allocation.", "Pressure carries it, because pressure integrates everything; water and gas are missing, because neither was requested.", "Water and gas carry it, because the model computed those; pressure is missing, because the history carries no pressure measurements.", "Nothing carries it at all, because under rate control every one of the observed quantities is imposed and none is left for the model to predict."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On a field with shut-in surveys the simulated pressure against the measured one is the strongest single test of a model. That Ekene has none is a limitation worth stating rather than discovering.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-reading-results', 15, 'A field water cut matches well and three of four wells do not. What does that mean?', '["The relative permeability curves are wrong, since a field match with per-well failures indicates one region where several are needed.", "The total water is right and it is coming from the wrong places, which is the most common and most informative pattern.", "The allocation behind the observed per-well water is wrong, since the field total is measured and the split is inferred.", "The model''s sweep is too poor, since compensating errors between wells always indicate an under-swept region somewhere."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It is invisible on a field plot, which is why the deck requests per-well water cut. Water arriving too early everywhere points at relative permeability or vertical communication; too late points at permeability or connectivity.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 1, 'Capstone field 1 asks for the side-track''s connection count. What are the two likely mistakes?', '["Counting only the connections that lie above the contact, and counting the layers the path descends through rather than the cells themselves.", "Counting distinct columns, which gives 8 and is field 2, or failing to merge re-entries, which gives more than the true count.", "Counting the connections the validator accepts rather than all of them, and including the heel cell twice at the start.", "Counting the columns times the layers, and counting the steps of the discretised walk rather than the merged connections."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The check is that the count must be at least the number of distinct columns and at most the columns times the layers. A path grazing a boundary produces alternating steps that must be collapsed into one connection per cell.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 2, 'Capstone field 2 asks for the distinct columns. What is the check?', '["It must equal the connection count minus the number of layers the path descends through, which is five here.", "It must be at least the horizontal distance divided by the cell size, since the path cannot skip a column it crosses.", "It must be strictly less than the connection count, because at least one column is entered in two layers.", "It must be even, because a path entering a column at one face and leaving at another always pairs its crossings."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The likely mistake is counting connections instead, which is field 1. Three columns on this trajectory are entered in one layer and left in the next.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 3, 'A learner bisects and the search runs to the edge of its bracket. What has gone wrong, and what else could?', '["Bisecting on the column-clipped volume, which converges elsewhere, or getting the search direction backwards, which diverges to a bracket edge.", "Bisecting on the oil cell count rather than the volume, or stopping the search before the residual falls below tolerance.", "Calibrating against the deck''s own volume rather than against the volumetric booking''s, or applying the calibration to the mapped contact depth instead.", "Bisecting in feet rather than metres, or bracketing the search around the contact rather than around the deepest well top."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tell that the sign is wrong is divergence to the bracket edge: shallower means MORE oil. The column-clipped convention gives more oil at the same mean, so it converges on a different value.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 4, 'What is the physical check on the calibrated regional mean?', '["It should sit above the shallowest well top, since a regional level below every well would give a structure that does not close.", "It should sit within one variogram range of the mean of the six well tops, since kriging reverts toward the data at that distance.", "It should sit at the depth of the midpoint between the crest and the contact, which is where a closing structure reverts to.", "It should sit below the deepest well top of 1590 m and above the contact at 1560 m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That is what a structure that closes looks like. A calibrated mean of 1450 m, shallower than every well, would have said the search or the objective was wrong rather than that the field is shallow.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 5, 'Capstone field 4 asks for the history oil total. What is the likely mistake and what is the check?', '["Summing the injection as well as the production, which roughly doubles the total; the check is that it matches the field''s booked recovery.", "A mean month of 30.4375 days instead of the real calendar month, which puts the total about a third of a percent high; the check is that it reproduces the RC4 ledger''s own oil sum.", "Summing only the producers that ran the whole history, which leaves the total low; the check is that all wells appear in all 36 periods.", "Summing rates rather than volumes, which gives a number in stb/d; the check is that the units come out in stock tank barrels."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The schedule was built from the ledger, so the round trip must return to it. A third of a percent is far outside the field''s tolerance.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 6, 'Capstone field 5 asks how many broken specifications the validator refuses. What is the likely mistake?', '["Counting the validator''s own rules rather than the broken cases, which is a larger number again since several of the rules are never exercised.", "Counting only the cases that raise exactly one message, which excludes the coupled case and gives six.", "Counting error messages rather than cases: one case raises two messages, so the message count is eight where the case count is seven.", "Counting the cases the composer also refuses, which is fewer since some broken specifications still produce deck text."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The check is that six of the cases raise exactly one message each. The seventh, the missing start date, is genuinely coupled to the rule that the first history period must match it.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 7, 'The datum depth is the mean of 900 numbers. Which two wrong means are a learner most likely to take?', '["Taking the mean of the layer bases rather than the tops, which is one net pay deeper, or taking the midpoint of the depth range rather than the mean of the values.", "Taking the crest depth rather than the mean, or taking the mean over the oil columns only rather than over all 900.", "Taking the contact depth, which is where a datum is conventionally set, or taking the depth of the deepest well.", "Taking the mean in metres rather than feet, or taking the mean of the six well tops rather than of the column tops."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The surface is not symmetric about its range, so the midpoint and the mean differ. The check is that the datum must sit inside the range of column tops, and on this field it falls below the contact, which surprises people.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 8, 'The equilibration datum is the mean of the 900 column tops at 5129.97013005754 ft. Why does it fall below the contact?', '["Because the mean is taken over the layer centres rather than the tops, which shifts it down by half a layer thickness.", "Because the datum is conventionally placed below the contact so that the equilibration solves downward through the whole of the oil column.", "Because the deepest columns are far more numerous than the shallow ones under a calibrated regional mean below the wells.", "Because more than half of the 900 columns are in the water leg, so the mean of a mostly-wet surface lands below the contact."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It sits about 12 ft below the contact. The datum is computed rather than chosen, which is why it lands wherever the surface''s mean lands rather than at a round number somebody picked.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 9, 'What do all six Expert capstone fields have in common?', '["Every one depends on the calibration, so an error in field 3 propagates into the other five and the tolerances allow for that.", "Every one is a computation on the deck rather than a reading of it, and every likely mistake is a defensible alternative computation.", "Every one of them can be obtained from the build explorer panel directly, so no arithmetic outside the panel itself is required for any of them.", "Every one has a counterpart at the Professional tier computed under a different convention, which is the comparison being tested."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Before submitting, for each answer, write down the rule you applied and the alternative you rejected. Trajectory mode gives fields 1 and 2 and validation mode gives field 5; the rest are computed.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 10, 'What did the tier learn from the first attempt at the validator cases?', '["That a validator should stop at the first error, since accumulating them makes a cascading case indistinguishable from an isolated one.", "That a specification should be broken by removing a field rather than by replacing one, since replacement invalidates the references to it.", "That a negative test can fail for a reason other than the one intended, so a suite of cascading cases can look thorough while exercising one rule repeatedly.", "That the error count is a better assertion than the message text, since messages change between versions while counts do not."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A case that raises 180 errors teaches the opposite of what it is for. The generator now asserts that no case raises more than two, so a future case that cascades fails the build rather than being described as isolated.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 11, 'What are the four things the course does not cover?', '["Running a deck, compositional fluids, well test analysis and the economics of a development decision.", "Corner-point geometry, faults, local grid refinement and dual porosity, which are the four grid features omitted.", "Running a deck, reading results, defining regions and choosing a control mode, every one of which needs a solver to be present.", "Running a deck, history matching as a practice, forecasting and uncertainty, and everything the grid cannot represent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Reading results IS covered, and ungraded. Corner-point geometry, faults, refinement and dual porosity are the contents of the fourth item rather than the list itself.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 12, 'Where does a Petrolord deck go to be run?', '["Any Eclipse-compatible simulator, since the emitter''s output is checked against the SPE1 reference for exactly that reason.", "The Suite''s Reservoir Simulation Studio, whose gate checks and operating rules are its own subject.", "The engines package''s own solver, which is invoked from the deck builder once the specification validates.", "Nowhere in the Petrolord platform; a deck built here is exported for a third-party simulator to run elsewhere."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course covers the deck-side half of deck security: knowing which keywords carry reach beyond the reservoir, so you recognise them in a deck you are sent and do not put them in one you send.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 13, 'Which of these did the course find about Ekene''s wells?', '["All six sit exactly on cell centres, and the residuals of about 2.3e-13 m are the only departure from an exact recovery.", "Five sit on cell centres and the sixth was deliberately withheld from the interpolation, which is why its predicted depth differs from the one in its log.", "Five sit exactly where the grid can honour them while Ekene-2 sits half a cell off the lattice and inherits a depth 0.68 m from its own log.", "All six sit off the lattice by varying amounts, and Ekene-2''s 0.68 m is simply the largest of the six gridding losses."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is a gridding loss rather than an interpolation loss, and no better interpolator fixes it. The picometre residuals on the exact wells are the metre to foot round trip rather than structure.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 14, 'What five steps does the tier recommend on an unfamiliar deck, in order?', '["Read the six sections and note their line ranges, run the four external comparisons, find what was calibrated, read the warnings once, and ask what would falsify it.", "Validate it, run it, compare the initial oil in place against the booking, read the warnings, and then plot the history match.", "Search it for scripting and inclusion keywords, validate it, read the warnings, run the four comparisons, and then calibrate.", "Read the RUNSPEC, check the grid against the map, check the fluid against the laboratory report, run it, and read the results."]'::jsonb, 0, 'If nothing was calibrated, ask why the model agrees with anything. If nothing could falsify it, the model is not making a claim.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 15, 'What is the argument for reading a deck?', '["It is the only artifact a simulator will accept, so any study that runs at all has one that can be inspected.", "It is the shortest complete description of a reservoir model, so reading one is faster than reading the study document.", "It is version-controlled alongside the code that produced it, so its history of changes is recoverable in a way a report''s is not.", "It is the most auditable artifact in the whole of reservoir engineering, because everything is in it and it is plain text."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The skill is not reading it. The skill is knowing which of its numbers are measurements, which are correlations, which are conventions and which are decisions, and being able to say so about each one.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'Why can a deviated well not be described by k1 and k2?', '["Because the pair carries no length information, and a deviated well''s connections differ considerably in how much wellbore each of them contains.", "Because the record format limits the pair to five layers, which a long lateral in a layered reservoir exceeds.", "Because those two numbers assume a vertical line through a single column, and a deviated path crosses columns as it descends.", "Because a deviated well may skip layers, and k1 to k2 always implies every layer between the two."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The set of cells a deviated path passes through is not a contiguous run of layers in anything, so the completion has to be a list, and producing that list is a geometry problem.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'How does an intersector turn a path into a connection list?', '["It walks the path in steps smaller than the smallest cell dimension, finds the cell each step''s midpoint falls in, and accumulates length per cell.", "It solves for the intersection of the path with each cell face analytically, which gives exact lengths per cell.", "It projects the path onto the map to get the columns, then assigns each column''s layer from the path''s depth at its centre.", "It tests the path against every cell''s bounding box, keeping the cells whose box the path enters and ordering them by depth."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Consecutive steps in the same cell are merged. On this grid the layers are the smallest dimension at about 6 ft, so the walk proceeds in steps of a foot or two.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'The Ekene side-track gives 11 connections across 8 distinct columns. What explains the difference?', '["Three connections are grazing artifacts, listed separately because the intersector could not merge them into their neighbours.", "Three of the eight columns carry two completions each, because the well was perforated over two separate intervals there.", "Three of the connections lie in the water leg, and those are listed in addition to the eight that carry oil.", "Three columns are entered in one layer and left in the next."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Columns (19, 20), (18, 21) and (17, 21) each appear twice, once in one layer and once in the next. The path runs from (20, 19) to (16, 22) descending through all five layers.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'Every one of the side-track''s connections is flagged X. Why?', '["Because the intersector reserves Z for wells given by k1 and k2, and flags every trajectory-derived connection X or Y.", "Because the path covers 500 m horizontally against about 10 m vertically, so the horizontal component dominates inside every cell it crosses.", "Because X is the default flag, and the intersector overrides it only where a path descends more than one layer in a single cell.", "Because the well''s overall azimuth is closer to east-west than to north-south, and the flag records that azimuth rather than the local behaviour of the path."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The intersector assigns the direction the path spent most of its length travelling in. X rather than Y because the path moves 400 m east-west against 300 m north-south, which is a genuinely close call.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'What does a connection wrongly flagged Z do on a field whose vertical permeability is a tenth of its horizontal?', '["It overstates the well index by roughly the square root of the anisotropy, because it uses two horizontal permeabilities where one should be vertical.", "It understates the well index by that same factor, because it takes the layer thickness as the flow length rather than the horizontal cell width.", "It has no effect unless the two horizontal permeabilities differ, since the formula is symmetric in them.", "It couples the well to the cells above and below as well, because Z connections are treated as spanning the layer interfaces."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Most simulators default the flag to Z when it is omitted, which makes it one of the most consequential defaults in a deck. A horizontal well written without flags is modelled as a stack of vertical penetrations.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'A trajectory enters the same cell twice. What does the intersector do?', '["It keeps both, because each represents a distinct interval of open wellbore with its own position in the sequence.", "It discards the second of the two visits, because the connection order is defined by first entry and a repeat would break that sequence.", "It merges the visits into one connection whose length is the total, because a cell has one pressure and the well one pressure there.", "It refuses the trajectory, because a path re-entering a cell indicates a survey integration error worth correcting."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two connections to the same cell would double-count the flow path. Simulators do accept duplicate COMPDAT records for a cell, and the result is exactly that double coupling.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'Which of the three ways a path can miss the grid is the dangerous one?', '["Outside the areal grid, because an empty connection list is silent and the well still appears in the schedule.", "Partially outside, because the connection list is simply shorter than intended and the deck validates.", "Above the top or below the base, because the map position looks correct and only the depths are wrong.", "None of them, because the validator refuses any well whose trajectory produces no connections at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Eleven connections where you expected eighteen is a valid deck, and the only person who can notice is somebody who knew what to expect. The validator does refuse a trajectory that misses entirely, naming the well.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'Under ORAT control, what is the model actually being tested on?', '["The water cut, the gas-oil ratio and the bottom-hole pressures, since the oil rate is imposed rather than matched.", "The oil rate, since the model must reproduce the observed volume within the tolerance the solver allows each step.", "The reservoir voidage, since honouring the oil rate fixes the total withdrawal once the formation volume factors apply.", "Nothing, since rate control leaves the model no freedom to be wrong about anything the schedule specifies."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A plot of modelled against observed oil rate under ORAT control is a straight line through the origin by definition, and it appears in more reports than it should.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'Which control mode makes reservoir voidage the exact quantity?', '["ORAT, since oil is the best-measured stream and voidage follows from it once Bo is applied.", "LRAT, since total liquid at reservoir conditions is what voidage measures.", "RESV.", "BHP, since fixing the bottom-hole pressure fixes the drawdown and therefore the total withdrawal."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each mode makes a different quantity exact and leaves the others free, so the choice decides what the match is testing. RESV is right when voidage is the quantity you trust, which is exactly the waterflood course''s ledger.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'Ekene-1 produced 998.5247575266284 stb in January 2023. What rate goes in the schedule?', '["32.804 stb/d, from dividing by the 30.4375-day mean month the prediction tail uses.", "32.210476049246076 stb/d, from dividing by January''s own 31 days.", "998.5247575266284 stb/d, since WCONHIST accepts the monthly volume when the periods are months.", "31.79 stb/d, from dividing by 31.4 days, the average month length over the calendar year 2023."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The days come from the actual calendar. A mean-month divisor gives a rate 1.8 percent high, which the simulator then applies over the 31 days the DATES block spans.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'The deck''s history oil total is 176923.83644033302 stb and the ledger''s is 176923.83644033293. What is the difference?', '["A rounding applied to one month''s rate on its way into the schedule, visible only in the last significant figures.", "The final period, which the deck closes with a date one month past the last month and the ledger does not.", "Floating-point addition order, since the ledger sums per well per month and the deck sums rate times days per period.", "A single well''s contribution in the final month, which the ledger allocates to it and the deck''s rate conversion does not."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Floating-point addition is not associative. A difference at the fifteenth significant figure is arithmetic and one at the fourth is a bug, which is the same distinction the Professional tier drew about Ekene-2''s depth.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'Why is a mean-month divisor''s error not removed by averaging over a year?', '["Because it follows the calendar: every 31-day month is over-produced and every 30-day month under-produced, with February out by nearly ten percent.", "Because the error compounds, since each month''s excess raises the starting cumulative the next month is measured from.", "Because production declines through the history, so the later and shorter months carry disproportionate weight.", "Because the prediction tail uses the same divisor, so the drift accumulated in history continues through the forecast."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The annual total drifts and the monthly pattern acquires a spurious seasonality. A history match whose residuals correlate with month length has this bug and nothing else.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'What defines the interval a rate block covers?', '["The DATES block that precedes it, which sets the time from which the rates take effect.", "The number of days named in the WCONHIST record itself, which is its last item.", "The report step length in force, since a rate block applies for exactly one report step.", "The DATES block that follows it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A rate block applies from the current time to the NEXT date. Reading it the other way round shifts the whole history by a month, which is nearly invisible on a cumulative plot and obvious on a rate plot.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'What does the validator enforce about the first history period?', '["It must be closed by a DATES block, since an unclosed first period would run into the prediction tail.", "Its date must equal the deck''s start date, and the message quotes both dates when it does not.", "It must name every well the model contains, since keyword persistence would otherwise leave wells uncontrolled.", "It must be no shorter than the first prediction step, so the simulator''s opening timestep is not truncated."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A mismatch is silent otherwise: the simulator would run from the start date with no wells until the first period, which is a shut-in field nobody asked for. Ekene starts 2023-01-01 and its first period is 2023-01-01.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'What separates a calibration from a validation?', '["A validation uses a measurement and a calibration uses another model''s output, so only one is grounded in data.", "A validation is performed once and a calibration is repeated whenever new data arrives during a study.", "A validation is a test the model could fail; a calibration is an adjustment it cannot fail, only be tuned through.", "A validation adjusts several parameters at once while a calibration adjusts exactly one of them against exactly one target."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both are legitimate. A study that calls a calibration a validation believes it has an independent check when it has none.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'What was tuned on this model, and against what?', '["The kriging regional mean, against the NG5 booking of 12139208.107496763 stock tank barrels.", "The contact depth, against the deepest oil and shallowest water seen in the six wells.", "The porosity, against the same booking, since porosity is the parameter the two models most directly share.", "The variogram range, against the residuals between the interpolated surface and the six mapped tops."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The regional mean is the depth the top surface reverts to far from any well. Nothing anywhere in the field''s dataset says what it should be, which makes it free in the strict sense.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'Why is tuning porosity to match a volume indefensible?', '["Because porosity and saturation enter the volume as a product, so a single target cannot separate the two.", "Because porosity is measured and appears in the booking too, so moving it overrides a measurement with a preference.", "Because the simulator recomputes porosity at initialisation from the pore volume, discarding any adjustment made.", "Because porosity is a per-cell property, and a single scalar adjustment cannot sensibly be applied to a mapped distribution."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rule is to calibrate the unconstrained parameter against the constrained quantity, never the reverse. An adjusted porosity would be wrong in every calculation that uses it, which is most of them.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'The calibrated regional mean is 1570.026311 m TVD, giving a gap of minus 0.056 percent. Why is the gap not zero?', '["Because the bisection was halted at a tolerance rather than run to machine precision, which would close it.", "Because the booking and the deck use different frames, so a residual of about one cell is irreducible by any search.", "Because the booking''s own value is rounded, so agreement closer than a tenth of a percent would be meaningless.", "Because the objective is a step function: columns and layers cross the contact one at a time."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The residual is the size of the smallest step the model can take rather than a failure of the search. Reporting the match as within a tenth of a percent rather than as an exact figure is the honest phrasing.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'Which three sanity checks does the tier apply to a bisected answer?', '["That it is physically reasonable, that it lies inside the bracket rather than at an edge, and that the volume responds in the expected direction.", "That it converged, that the residual is under tolerance, and that a second search from a wider bracket agrees with it.", "That it honours the six well tops, that it respects the contact, and that the resulting surface closes structurally.", "That it is deeper than the contact, shallower than the deepest well, and inside the mapped area of the field."]'::jsonb, 0, '1570 m is about 10 m below the contact and a little below the deepest well at 1590 m. A calibrated mean of 1400 m, shallower than every well, would have signalled that something else was wrong.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'The volumes agree and the cell counts are 169 against 266. What produced that?', '["The deck uses a finer frame, so the same accumulation is described by proportionally more cells.", "The deck counts every column carrying any oil while the booking counts only those carrying the full five layers.", "The booking clips area at the contact with a full isochore everywhere; the deck clips by cell centre, so most of its columns are partial.", "The deck includes the two injector columns, which the booking excluded as lying below the mapped contact."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Matching the total means the larger area compensates for the thinner average column. One equation and one unknown: fix the volume and the area follows.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'Calibrating on cell count instead would give exactly 169 oil cells. What would the model hold?', '["Roughly the booked volume, since matching area with the same isochore reproduces the same integral.", "Roughly half the booked volume, because most of those 169 cells would be partial columns rather than full ones.", "Roughly twice the booked volume, since a tighter structure concentrates the oil into fuller columns at the crest.", "The same volume, since on a monotonic surface the two objectives have the same solution."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every forecast from that model would be low by a factor of two, because a forecast is a fraction of the oil in place. That is the decisive argument for matching volume.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'Take the validator''s rules as a set. What is true of every one of them?', '["Each is cross-referential, checking one part of the specification against another rather than in isolation.", "Each is arithmetic, so the validator needs no knowledge of what any keyword means to the simulator.", "Each corresponds to an error the simulator itself would raise, so a validated deck is one it will accept.", "Each is about whether the deck can be built and read, not whether it is right."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It will accept a grid in the wrong place, a fluid from the wrong field, a contact 100 ft too deep and a history from a different well, provided each is structurally well formed.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'Why can a deck builder refuse where a simulator must warn?', '["Because a builder is upstream with a specification in hand, so refusing costs the caller only a corrected input, while a simulator receives decks from everywhere.", "Because a builder runs before deck text exists, and a warning would have nothing to point at until it does.", "Because a simulator''s warnings concern numerical behaviour during the run, which a builder cannot anticipate at all.", "Because a builder serves one specification format while a simulator must accept several, and warnings absorb the differences."]'::jsonb, 0, 'If a simulator refused everything unusual it would refuse most real decks. So it warns and continues, and the burden moves to the reader.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'The fixture''s seven broken specifications each isolate one rule. Why does that matter?', '["Because the validator checks its rules in sequence, so a case tripping an early rule would prevent the later ones running.", "Because a case raising several messages leaves their order undefined, which makes an assertion on the message fragile.", "Because seven cases each raising one error demonstrates seven independent rules, while seven cases raising a hundred errors demonstrates only that broken things are broken.", "Because a specification with more than one fault cannot be repaired one step at a time, which the exercise requires."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The set exists to say something about the validator rather than about how badly a specification can be mangled.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'Which of the seven cases raises two errors, and is that a flaw?', '["The well outside the grid, and it is not: invalid indices genuinely also put the completion outside the layers.", "The missing start date, and it is not: a deck with no start date genuinely also fails the rule that the first history period must match it.", "The layer count mismatch, and it is a flaw: the case should have been written to drop a single layer entry rather than to change the count itself.", "The single-node PVT table, and it is: the table rule and the datum pressure rule should not have been coupled."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two rules depend on the same field. The generator asserts that no case raises more than two errors, so a future case that cascades fails the build rather than being described as isolated.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'The well cases first raised 180 errors each. What was the cause and the general lesson?', '["The whole wells list had been replaced by one modified well, so the 36-period history named five absent wells; the lesson is to check each negative test fails for the reason you think.", "The trajectory intersector ran before the grid was validated, so every connection fell outside; the lesson is to order validation rules by dependency.", "The error accumulator was not reset between cases, so each inherited its predecessors'' errors; the lesson is that shared state makes tests unreliable.", "The completion index exceeded the layer count as well, so both well rules fired in every period; the lesson is to break one field at a time."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A cascading case still fails, so a suite of them still passes, and nobody notices that six of the seven rules are never exercised on their own.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'A simulator distinguishes the two. Which of these would it raise as a warning?', '["A completion below the deepest layer, since the well is declared and the run can proceed without that connection.", "A history period dated before the one preceding it, since the simulator can sort the periods itself.", "A table extrapolated beyond its range and held at the end value.", "A missing datum pressure, since equilibration can proceed from the datum depth and the contacts alone."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A warning means the deck can be processed and something about it is unusual. Extrapolation is legal, and it is exactly the class that changes an answer materially without stopping anything.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'Which category of error does no external comparison catch?', '["A history reproducing a production database exactly, where the database''s own allocation was wrong.", "A PVT table taken from a correlation where the rest of the study used a designed fluid description.", "A structure with correct relief and the crest in the wrong corner, which every summary statistic matches.", "A model right in every respect built on a field that does not behave as it assumes."]'::jsonb, 3, 'No crossflow where there is crossflow, one saturation region where there are three, a single tank where there are two compartments. Only a prediction that fails surfaces it, which is an argument for making predictions that can fail.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'Why is the results module ungraded?', '["Because results depend on which simulator was used, so no single answer could be marked correct across learners.", "Because the engines emit decks and do geometry rather than solving flow, so the course has no simulated result to check an answer against.", "Because reading is a qualitative skill, and the capstone chassis can only grade numeric fields within a tolerance.", "Because the module sits outside the five the tier''s capstone draws its six fields from."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A course should grade what it can check and teach what a practitioner needs. Extending the graded scope to simulated results would mean grading against numbers this course cannot compute.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'Ekene''s deck writes 36 history steps and 60 prediction steps. What does its summary hold?', '["96 rows, one per report step, each holding the state at the end of that step.", "96 rows, one per report step, each holding the state at the solver step nearest that report time.", "36 rows, since the prediction tail advances on TSTEP and is written to a separate file from the history.", "A variable number, since the simulator adds a row whenever it cuts a timestep to achieve convergence."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The solver''s own internal steps do not appear. A report step may have taken twenty solver steps or one, and the summary shows the state at the end of it either way.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'Why does differencing a cumulative reproduce a rate exactly?', '["Because the solver accumulates both from the same internal steps, so the two are algebraically identical at every report time.", "Because a cumulative is written at the end of the step and a rate at its midpoint, so the two align by construction.", "Because a reported rate is the average over the step, which is precisely the change in the total divided by the step length.", "It does not; the two agree only when the steps are short enough for the rate to be nearly constant across them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reading a rate as an instantaneous value is the approximation, and it gets worse as steps get longer. If a rate and a total disagree in a way that matters, trust the total.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'What is the first thing to read in a run''s output?', '["The field oil production total at the final report step, which is what the study exists to forecast.", "The warning count, which shows how much of the deck the simulator had to interpret on its own.", "The average field pressure at the end of the history, where a material balance error would first show.", "The oil in place at initialisation."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is the denominator of every recovery factor the study will quote and the first place a static-model error shows up. Reading it against the volumetric booking takes ten seconds.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'Decode WOPRH and FGIT.', '["Well oil production rate at the heel, and field gas in place total.", "Observed well oil production rate, and field gas injection total to date.", "Well oil production rate history-matched, and field gross injection total.", "Well oil production reservoir-condition rate, and field gas injection target."]'::jsonb, 1, 'First letter is the scope, then the quantity, then a trailing H for the observed value rather than the simulated one. The H vectors are the numbers you put in the schedule, echoed back.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'What does a plot of FOPR against FOPRH show under ORAT control, and why?', '["Two identical curves, because the model was told the oil rate, so the plot demonstrates nothing about the match.", "Two curves agreeing where the model matches and diverging where it does not, which is what makes the plot useful.", "One curve, because the simulator writes the observed vector only where it differs from the simulated one.", "Two curves diverging late, because the imposed rate becomes unachievable once the reservoir pressure has fallen."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That plot appears in a great many reports as evidence of a good history match. What carries information is what the model was NOT told: water, gas, and on a field with surveys, pressure.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'A field water cut matches and three of four wells do not. What does that pattern mean?', '["The observed per-well split is an allocation and only the field total is measured, so the per-well mismatch is expected.", "The relative permeability needs more than one saturation region, since a single curve cannot match wells that differ.", "The total water is right and it is coming from the wrong places, which is the most common and most informative pattern.", "The model''s sweep is too poor, since compensating per-well errors always indicate an under-swept region."]'::jsonb, 2, 'It is invisible on a field plot, which is why the deck requests per-well water cut as well. Water arriving too early everywhere points at relative permeability or vertical communication instead.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'What is the likely mistake on the capstone''s connection count, and the check?', '["Counting the individual steps of the discretised walk instead, which gives several hundred; the check is that the count stays under the layer count.", "Counting distinct columns instead, which gives 8; the check is that the count is at least the column count and at most columns times layers.", "Counting only the connections in the oil leg, which gives fewer; the check is that every layer appears at least once.", "Counting the columns times the layers, which gives 40; the check is that the count is a multiple of the layer count."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Failing to merge re-entries is the other mistake, and it gives more than the true count because a path grazing a boundary produces alternating steps that must be collapsed.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'A bisection on the regional mean runs to the edge of its bracket. What does that tell you?', '["The bracket was too narrow, and the true answer lies outside it in the direction the search ran.", "The objective is not monotonic over that interval, so a bisection cannot be applied to it at all.", "The target volume is unreachable at any regional mean, so another parameter must be adjusted as well.", "The search direction is backwards, since shallower means more oil."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Divergence to a bracket edge is the tell that the sign is wrong. A bracket that is too narrow shows as an answer AT an edge rather than a search that runs to it, which is why the tier checks for interiority separately.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'What is the check on the capstone''s history oil total?', '["That it reproduces the RC4 ledger''s own oil sum exactly, because the schedule was built from that ledger.", "That it matches the booked oil in place multiplied by the field''s expected recovery factor over three years.", "That it equals the sum of the 36 monthly volumes divided by the mean month length, in stock tank barrels.", "That it exceeds the injection total over the same period, since the field''s voidage ratio is above one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The likely mistake is a mean month of 30.4375 days instead of the real calendar month, which puts the total about a third of a percent high and far outside the tolerance.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'How many messages do the seven validator cases raise between them, and why does it matter?', '["Seven, because each case isolates exactly one rule; it matters because a different count would mean a cascade.", "Fourteen, because each case raises one error and one warning; it matters because only the errors are counted.", "Eight, because one case is genuinely coupled to a second rule; it matters because the capstone asks for cases rather than messages.", "It varies with the order the cases run in; it matters because the capstone tolerance has to accommodate that."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Six of the cases raise exactly one message each. Counting messages rather than cases is the likely mistake on that field.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'The equilibration datum is the mean of the 900 column tops. What are the two wrong means?', '["The mean over the oil columns only, and the mean of the six well tops rather than of the interpolated surface.", "The mean of the layer bases, one net pay deeper, and the midpoint of the depth range, which differs because the surface is not symmetric about its range.", "The mean in metres rather than feet, and the mean weighted by cell volume rather than taken over columns equally.", "The crest depth, which is the shallowest rather than the mean, and the contact depth, which is where a datum is often set."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The datum is 5129.97013005754 ft, about 12 ft below the contact, because more than half the columns are in the water leg. The check is that it must sit inside the range of column tops.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'What is the general advice for approaching the tier''s six graded fields?', '["Each is a computation on the deck rather than a reading of it, and each likely mistake is a defensible alternative computation.", "Each can be read directly from the build explorer panel, so the capstone tests navigation rather than arithmetic.", "Each depends on the calibrated regional mean, so an error in that field propagates into the other five.", "Each has a Professional-tier counterpart computed under a different clipping convention, and the pair of them is the comparison being tested."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Before submitting, for each answer, write down the rule you applied and the alternative you rejected. Trajectory mode gives two of the fields and validation mode gives one; the rest are computed.'),
  ('sim', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'What are the five steps the tier recommends on an unfamiliar deck?', '["Validate it, run it, check the initial oil in place against the booking, read the warnings, and plot the history match.", "Grep it for scripting and inclusion keywords, validate it, read the warnings, run the four comparisons, and recalibrate.", "Read the RUNSPEC, check the grid against the map, check the fluid against the laboratory report, run it, read the results.", "Read the six sections and note their line ranges, run the four external comparisons, find what was calibrated, read the warnings once, and ask what would falsify it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'If nothing was calibrated, ask why the model agrees with anything. If nothing could falsify it, the model is not making a claim. The security grep is worth doing on a deck from outside and it is not one of the five.');
